This version treats the answer as a boundary between values smaller than the target and values greater than or equal to it. The recursive range uses `right` as an exclusive limit, which makes the empty array case natural.

For `[1, 3, 5, 6]` and target `2`, the calls narrow the range from `[0, 4)` to `[0, 2)` and then to `[1, 2)`. The final range starts at index `1`, so that is returned.

```go
func searchInsert(nums []int, target int) int {
    var find func(int, int) int
    find = func(left int, right int) int {
        if left == right {
            return left
        }

        mid := left + (right-left)/2
        if nums[mid] < target {
            return find(mid+1, right)
        }
        return find(left, mid)
    }

    return find(0, len(nums))
}
```

The input slice is not modified. The extra space comes from the recursive calls, unlike an iterative binary search.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of recursion

`lower_bound` searches for the first element that is not smaller than `target`. If the target exists, this gives its index. If it does not, the returned iterator is the exact place where it can be inserted.

This is short, but it depends on knowing the standard library function. The vector remains unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int searchInsert(vector<int> &nums, int target)  {
        auto position = lower_bound(nums.begin(), nums.end(), target);
        return static_cast<int>(position - nums.begin());
    }
};
```

For `nums = [1, 3, 5, 6]` and target `4`, the iterator points to `5`, at index `2`. For an empty vector, both iterators are equal and the result is `0`.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

The array is sorted, so the first value greater than or equal to `target` is the answer. If every value is smaller, the target belongs at index `nums.size()`.

This is not the fastest method for a large array, but it is very easy to follow. It also leaves the input unchanged.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int searchInsert(vector<int> &nums, int target)  {
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            if (nums[i] >= target) {
                return i;
            }
        }
        return nums.size();
    }
};
```

For `nums = [1, 3, 5, 6]` and `target = 2`, the scan stops at `3`, so the returned index is `1`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

I used the usual binary search with `left` and `right`. When the middle value is smaller than the target, the answer must be to its right. Otherwise, the middle index may still be the insertion position, so the right side moves left.

When the pointers cross, `left` is the first place where the target can be inserted. The midpoint calculation also avoids adding the two boundaries directly.

```java
class Solution {
    public int searchInsert(int[] nums, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] == target) {
                return mid;
            } else if (nums[mid] < target) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
}
```

A target smaller than the first value returns `0`. A target larger than the last value moves `left` to `nums.length`, so insertion at the end is handled too.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

